(*
# Dump Item Definitions
Produces `jsons/item-definitions.zip`: one Gson-pretty-printed JSON document per
item of the CONFIGS/ITEM archive, named `<id>.json`.

This is a **byte-parity** artifact. The Java it replaces is
`SimbaItemDumper` -> `ItemExporter.export()` -> `new GsonBuilder()
.setPrettyPrinting().create().toJson(item)`, and the committed archive is
*unlinked* - `SimbaItemDumper.link()` is never called by `dumpItems`, so item 7
still ships `"name": "null"` next to `notedTemplate: 799`. Raw
`TItemDefinition.Load` output is therefore directly comparable, and
`dev/tests/411_itemdefs.simba` holds all 33745 entries to byte equality.

## What Gson's serialiser does, and why each rule matters

1. **Field order is Java declaration order**, not alphabetical and not cache
   order: `ItemDefinition.java:34-112`. Gson reflects over the class in
   declaration order and writes what it finds.
2. **Null object fields are omitted entirely.** No `serializeNulls`, so
   `examine`, `unknown1`, `colorFind`, `colorReplace`, `textureFind`,
   `textureReplace`, `countCo`, `countObj`, `subops` and `params` appear only
   when the Java field is non-null. Nulls *inside* arrays are still written -
   `interfaceOptions`, `groundOps.ops` and `subops` all carry them.
3. **Pretty printing** is a 2-space indent per level, `": "` between key and
   value, `\n` newlines, `[]`/`{}` for an empty container, and **no trailing
   newline** at the end of the document.
4. **HTML-safe escaping is ON**, because the instance came from `GsonBuilder`.
   The five characters `&` `'` `<` `=` `>` go out as their `\u00xx` long form -
   3536 apostrophes across 2800 files in the committed archive, and not one raw
   one anywhere. See {ref}`JSONEscapeHTML`. Non-ASCII is raw UTF-8: only `a`
   grave, `e` acute and `U+2122` occur, in 7 files.
5. **Never emit a float.** Every value in this archive is an integer, a boolean
   or a string; a port that routes an int through a Double and writes `1.0`
   loses parity silently.
6. **Shorts are signed.** Opcodes 40/41 store `(short) readUnsignedShort()`, so
   item 22 reads 43968 off the wire and this writes `-21568`. 4199 items have
   palette entries at or above `$8000`. The loader already stores them signed;
   this unit must not "fix" them.

## params is in Java HashMap bucket order
`ItemLoader` builds a `HashMap<Integer,Object>` and `ItemExporter` hands it
straight to Gson, so the committed key order is the map's *iteration* order:

* table size starts at 16 with threshold `0.75n`; after inserting the k-th
  entry, `k > threshold` doubles both.
* bucket = `(h ^ (h >>> 16)) and (n-1)`, and for an `Integer` key `h` is the
  value itself.
* buckets ascending, and **within a bucket, insertion order** - which is cache
  order, *not* ascending key order. Java 8 preserves relative order across a
  resize, so the chain never gets reshuffled.

Item **6523** is the case that discriminates the two: it emits param 434 before
param 2 and both land in bucket 2 at `n=16`, so ascending-within-bucket writes
them the wrong way round. Measured over the committed archive, the bucket model
reproduces 11711/11711 param orders; assuming ascending insertion reproduces
11244. `TCacheParams`' parallel arrays already carry cache order, which is
exactly what the model needs.

## The one thing the loader cannot tell you: "" versus null
`TItemDefinition` stores `''` for both a Java `null` string and a genuinely
empty one (see `defs/item.simba`, divergence 3). That is not academic here:
**243 of the 33745 committed entries ship `"examine": ""`** rather than omitting
the key, and item 2749 ships an empty `name` as well. Emitting on `Examine <> ''`
alone therefore costs 243 entries.

{ref}`ItemDefJSONOf` resolves it by asking the loader instead of guessing, with
no second opcode table to keep in sync: when a string field decodes empty it
re-runs `TItemDefinition.Load` over `03 2A 00 09 2A 00` + the original bytes.
Those six bytes are two complete, well-formed opcodes - opcode 3 (`examine`) and
opcode 9 (`unknown1`), each carrying the one-character string `*` - so the
stream stays exactly consumed and every other field decodes identically. A real
opcode later in the data overwrites the sentinel; no opcode leaves it standing.
The sentinel can never be mistaken for a real value, because the probe is only
consulted for fields whose real value is already known to be `''`.

`name` needs none of this: `ItemDefinition.name` is initialised to `"null"` and
is never null, so it is always written.

## Emitted but never exercised on this cache
`groundOps.subOps`, `groundOps.conditionalOps` and `groundOps.conditionalSubOps`
are `[]` for every one of the 33745 committed entries, so the reconstruction
below is written from `EntityOpsDefinition.java` rather than measured. Two
assumptions are baked in and would need revisiting if a cache revision starts
emitting them: `TEntityOps` keeps one entry per `(index, subId)` where Java's
list keeps duplicates in insertion order, and this writes sub-ops in ascending
`subId` where Java writes them in insertion order.
*)
{$DEFINE WL_CACHE_DUMP_ITEMDEFS_INCLUDED}
{$IFNDEF WL_CACHE_DUMP_COMMON_INCLUDED}{$INCLUDE_ONCE dumpcommon.simba}{$ENDIF}
{$IFNDEF WL_CACHE_DUMP_ZIPWRITER_INCLUDED}{$INCLUDE_ONCE zipwriter.simba}{$ENDIF}
{$IFNDEF WL_CACHE_TYPES_INCLUDED}{$INCLUDE_ONCE ../cachetypes.simba}{$ENDIF}
{$IFNDEF WL_CACHE_STORE_INCLUDED}{$INCLUDE_ONCE ../store.simba}{$ENDIF}
{$IFNDEF WL_CACHE_DEF_ITEM_INCLUDED}{$INCLUDE_ONCE ../defs/item.simba}{$ENDIF}

const
  // The probe prefix and its marker, see the docstring. Byte 42 is '*'.
  ITEMDEF_PROBE_BYTE: Integer = 42;
  ITEMDEF_PROBE_MARK: String = '*';

// 2 spaces per level. A local typed const is static in Lape - it is not rebuilt
// per call - and nesting never gets past 5 here, so the loop is a safety net
// rather than a path anything takes.
function _JsonIndent(depth: Integer): String;
const
  LEVELS: TStringArray = ['', '  ', '    ', '      ', '        ', '          ',
                          '            '];
begin
  if (depth >= 0) and (depth <= High(LEVELS)) then Exit(LEVELS[depth]);
  Result := '';
  while depth > 0 do
  begin
    Result += '  ';
    Dec(depth);
  end;
end;

type
(*
## TJsonPretty
```pascal
TJsonPretty = record
  Text: String;
  Depth: Integer;
  Fresh: Boolean;
end;
```
A cursor that writes Gson's pretty form directly into one string, so nothing is
built into intermediate arrays and thrown away. `Fresh` is "nothing has been
written inside the container we are in yet", which is what decides between a
leading newline and a leading comma - and what makes an untouched container come
out as `{}` / `[]` rather than `{\n}`.

No stack is needed for `Fresh`. Opening a container is always preceded by a
{ref}`TJsonPretty.Key` or a {ref}`TJsonPretty.Sep`, both of which clear the
parent's `Fresh`, so closing one can restore it unconditionally to False.

Appending with `+=` is deliberate: measured on this build, Lape appends in place
and beats a preallocated buffer with manual growth.
*)
  TJsonPretty = record
    Text: String;
    Depth: Integer;
    Fresh: Boolean;
  end;

// Starts a fresh document. A record local is not zero-filled per call, so this
// has to clear all three fields rather than just Text.
procedure TJsonPretty.Init();
begin
  Self.Text := '';
  Self.Depth := 0;
  Self.Fresh := False;
end;

(*
## TJsonPretty.Sep
```pascal
procedure TJsonPretty.Sep();
```
Opens the next member or element of the current container: the comma if one is
needed, then the newline and this level's indent.
*)
procedure TJsonPretty.Sep();
begin
  if Self.Fresh then Self.Text += #10
  else Self.Text += ',' + #10;
  Self.Text += _JsonIndent(Self.Depth);
  Self.Fresh := False;
end;

(*
## TJsonPretty.Key
```pascal
procedure TJsonPretty.Key(name: String);
```
Writes `"name": ` as the next member of the current object. `name` is written
verbatim - every key in this artifact is a bare identifier or a decimal number.
*)
procedure TJsonPretty.Key(name: String);
begin
  Self.Sep();
  Self.Text += '"' + name + '": ';
end;

(*
## TJsonPretty.Field
```pascal
procedure TJsonPretty.Field(name, value: String);
```
One `"name": value` member, where `value` is already-rendered JSON.
*)
procedure TJsonPretty.Field(name, value: String);
begin
  Self.Key(name);
  Self.Text += value;
end;

(*
## TJsonPretty.Elem
```pascal
procedure TJsonPretty.Elem(value: String);
```
One array element, where `value` is already-rendered JSON.
*)
procedure TJsonPretty.Elem(value: String);
begin
  Self.Sep();
  Self.Text += value;
end;

procedure TJsonPretty.OpenObj();
begin
  Self.Text += '{';
  Inc(Self.Depth);
  Self.Fresh := True;
end;

procedure TJsonPretty.CloseObj();
begin
  Dec(Self.Depth);
  if Self.Fresh then Self.Text += '}'
  else Self.Text += #10 + _JsonIndent(Self.Depth) + '}';
  Self.Fresh := False;
end;

procedure TJsonPretty.OpenArr();
begin
  Self.Text += '[';
  Inc(Self.Depth);
  Self.Fresh := True;
end;

procedure TJsonPretty.CloseArr();
begin
  Dec(Self.Depth);
  if Self.Fresh then Self.Text += ']'
  else Self.Text += #10 + _JsonIndent(Self.Depth) + ']';
  Self.Fresh := False;
end;

(*
## JSONQuoteHTML
```pascal
function JSONQuoteHTML(s: String): String;
```
`s` as a JSON string literal, quotes included, escaped the HTML-safe way this
artifact uses.
*)
function JSONQuoteHTML(s: String): String;
begin
  Result := '"' + JSONEscapeHTML(s) + '"';
end;

function _JsonBool(b: Boolean): String;
begin
  if b then Result := 'true' else Result := 'false';
end;

// A `short[]` / `int[]` field: one number per line, `[]` when empty. Only ever
// reached for a non-null Java array, since a null one omits the key entirely.
procedure _JsonIntArray(var j: TJsonPretty; arr: TIntegerArray);
var
  i: Integer;
begin
  j.OpenArr();
  for i := 0 to High(arr) do
    j.Elem(ToStr(arr[i]));
  j.CloseArr();
end;

// A fixed-slot `String[]` - interfaceOptions, and one row of subops. An unset
// slot is Java's null, which this port stores as ''.
procedure _JsonSlotArray(var j: TJsonPretty; arr: TStringArray);
var
  i: Integer;
begin
  j.OpenArr();
  for i := 0 to High(arr) do
    if arr[i] = '' then j.Elem('null')
    else j.Elem(JSONQuoteHTML(arr[i]));
  j.CloseArr();
end;

(*
## ItemDefParamOrder
```pascal
procedure ItemDefParamOrder(params: TCacheParams; var order: TIntegerArray);
```
Fills `order` with positions into `params`' parallel arrays, in the order a Java
`HashMap<Integer,Object>` built by inserting them in cache order would iterate.
See the unit docstring for the model; this is the whole of it.

Duplicate ids are collapsed the way `HashMap.put` collapses them - the **first**
occurrence fixes the position in the chain, the **last** supplies the value - so
`order` can be shorter than `params.Ids`. No item in cache 2625 has a duplicate
param id, so that branch is reasoned from `HashMap`, not measured.

Treeification is not modelled: a bucket would need 8 entries in a table of at
least 64, and the largest param block in the archive is 53 entries spread over
128 buckets.
*)
procedure ItemDefParamOrder(params: TCacheParams; var order: TIntegerArray);
var
  uniqIds, uniqPos, buckets: TIntegerArray;
  i, k, n, h, table, threshold: Integer;
  found: Integer;
begin
  order := [];
  n := Length(params.Ids);
  if n = 0 then Exit;

  // Dedupe into insertion order. n is at most 53 here, so the linear scan is
  // cheaper than anything with a table behind it.
  for i := 0 to n - 1 do
  begin
    found := -1;
    for k := 0 to High(uniqIds) do
      if uniqIds[k] = params.Ids[i] then
      begin
        found := k;
        Break;
      end;
    if found = -1 then
    begin
      uniqIds += params.Ids[i];
      uniqPos += i;
    end
    else
      uniqPos[found] := i;  // HashMap.put keeps the last value in the first slot
  end;

  // HashMap's default constructor: capacity 16, load factor 0.75, and
  // `if (++size > threshold) resize()` doubling both.
  table := 16;
  threshold := 12;
  for k := 1 to Length(uniqIds) do
    if k > threshold then
    begin
      table := table * 2;
      threshold := table * 3 div 4;
    end;

  SetLength(buckets, Length(uniqIds));
  for k := 0 to High(uniqIds) do
  begin
    // HashMap.hash: h ^ (h >>> 16), with h = Integer.hashCode = the value.
    // Param ids come off the wire as a 24-bit unsigned, so h is never negative
    // and the mask only guards against a future signed one.
    h := uniqIds[k];
    buckets[k] := (h xor ((h shr 16) and $FFFF)) and (table - 1);
  end;

  // Buckets ascending, insertion order preserved within each - which is exactly
  // what walking the table and following each chain gives.
  for i := 0 to table - 1 do
    for k := 0 to High(uniqIds) do
      if buckets[k] = i then
        order += uniqPos[k];
end;

// EntityOpsDefinition.ops is a List that setOp grows to `index + 1`, so its
// length is the highest slot ever written plus one - and never less than 3,
// because ItemDefinition seeds ground op 2 with "Take" at construction.
function _ItemGroundOpsLength(ops: TStringArray): Integer;
var
  i: Integer;
begin
  Result := 3;
  for i := Result to High(ops) do
    if ops[i] <> '' then Result := i + 1;
end;

// groundOps.subOps / conditionalOps / conditionalSubOps. See the unit
// docstring: every committed entry has all three empty, so this is a port of
// EntityOpsDefinition rather than a measured shape.
procedure _ItemSubOps(var j: TJsonPretty; ops: TEntityOps);
var
  rows, i, k: Integer;
begin
  rows := 0;
  for i := 0 to High(ops.SubOps) do
    for k := 0 to High(ops.SubOps[i]) do
      if ops.SubOps[i][k] <> '' then
      begin
        rows := i + 1;
        Break;
      end;

  j.OpenArr();
  for i := 0 to rows - 1 do
  begin
    j.Sep();
    j.OpenArr();
    for k := 0 to High(ops.SubOps[i]) do
      if ops.SubOps[i][k] <> '' then
      begin
        j.Sep();
        j.OpenObj();
        // Gson walks the declared class before its superclass, so SubOp's own
        // subID precedes Op's text.
        j.Field('subID', ToStr(k));
        j.Field('text', JSONQuoteHTML(ops.SubOps[i][k]));
        j.CloseObj();
      end;
    j.CloseArr();
  end;
  j.CloseArr();
end;

// One ConditionalOp/ConditionalSubOp body. `withSubId` picks the subclass:
// ConditionalSubOp declares subID, then ConditionalOp's four fields, then Op's
// text - Gson's subclass-before-superclass order again.
procedure _ItemConditionalBody(var j: TJsonPretty; op: TEntityConditionalOp; withSubId: Boolean);
begin
  j.OpenObj();
  if withSubId then j.Field('subID', ToStr(op.SubId));
  j.Field('varpID', ToStr(op.Varp));
  j.Field('varbitID', ToStr(op.Varb));
  j.Field('minValue', ToStr(op.Min));
  j.Field('maxValue', ToStr(op.Max));
  j.Field('text', JSONQuoteHTML(op.Text));
  j.CloseObj();
end;

procedure _ItemConditionalOps(var j: TJsonPretty; ops: array of TEntityConditionalOp);
var
  rows, i, k: Integer;
begin
  rows := 0;
  for i := 0 to High(ops) do
    if ops[i].Index + 1 > rows then rows := ops[i].Index + 1;

  j.OpenArr();
  for i := 0 to rows - 1 do
  begin
    j.Sep();
    j.OpenArr();
    for k := 0 to High(ops) do
      if ops[k].Index = i then
      begin
        j.Sep();
        _ItemConditionalBody(j, ops[k], False);
      end;
    j.CloseArr();
  end;
  j.CloseArr();
end;

// conditionalSubOps is a List of HashMaps padded with **null**, not with empty
// maps - the one place EntityOpsDefinition pads differently.
procedure _ItemConditionalSubOps(var j: TJsonPretty; ops: array of TEntityConditionalOp);
var
  rows, i, k, m, table, threshold, h, s: Integer;
  subIds, buckets: TIntegerArray;
  any, known: Boolean;
begin
  rows := 0;
  for i := 0 to High(ops) do
    if ops[i].Index + 1 > rows then rows := ops[i].Index + 1;

  j.OpenArr();
  for i := 0 to rows - 1 do
  begin
    any := False;
    subIds := [];
    for k := 0 to High(ops) do
      if ops[k].Index = i then
      begin
        any := True;
        // First appearance fixes the key's place in the map's chain.
        known := False;
        for s := 0 to High(subIds) do
          if subIds[s] = ops[k].SubId then
          begin
            known := True;
            Break;
          end;
        if not known then subIds += ops[k].SubId;
      end;

    if not any then
    begin
      j.Elem('null');
      Continue;
    end;

    table := 16;
    threshold := 12;
    for k := 1 to Length(subIds) do
      if k > threshold then
      begin
        table := table * 2;
        threshold := table * 3 div 4;
      end;
    SetLength(buckets, Length(subIds));
    for k := 0 to High(subIds) do
    begin
      h := subIds[k];
      buckets[k] := (h xor ((h shr 16) and $FFFF)) and (table - 1);
    end;

    j.Sep();
    j.OpenObj();
    for m := 0 to table - 1 do
      for k := 0 to High(subIds) do
        if buckets[k] = m then
        begin
          j.Key(ToStr(subIds[k]));
          j.OpenArr();
          for h := 0 to High(ops) do
            if (ops[h].Index = i) and (ops[h].SubId = subIds[k]) then
            begin
              j.Sep();
              _ItemConditionalBody(j, ops[h], True);
            end;
          j.CloseArr();
        end;
    j.CloseObj();
  end;
  j.CloseArr();
end;

(*
## ItemDefJSON
```pascal
function ItemDefJSON(item: TItemDefinition; hasExamine, hasUnknown1: Boolean): String;
```
One item as `ItemExporter` would have written it: Gson pretty form, HTML-safe
escaping, fields in `ItemDefinition.java` declaration order, no trailing
newline.

`hasExamine` / `hasUnknown1` say whether the cache actually carried opcode 3 /
opcode 9, which the record itself cannot answer - `''` covers both "absent" and
"present and empty", and 243 items really do ship `"examine": ""`. Pass what
{ref}`ItemDefJSONOf` works out, or compute it the same way.

```pascal
WriteLn(ItemDefJSON(defs.Item(4151), True, False));
```
*)
function ItemDefJSON(item: TItemDefinition; hasExamine, hasUnknown1: Boolean): String;
var
  j: TJsonPretty;
  order: TIntegerArray;
  i, k, n: Integer;
begin
  j.Init();
  j.OpenObj();

  j.Field('id', ToStr(item.Id));
  // ItemDefinition.name starts at "null" and is never null, so it is always
  // written - including for the 1-item case where the cache set it to "".
  j.Field('name', JSONQuoteHTML(item.Name));
  if hasExamine then j.Field('examine', JSONQuoteHTML(item.Examine));
  if hasUnknown1 then j.Field('unknown1', JSONQuoteHTML(item.Unknown1));

  j.Field('resizeX', ToStr(item.ResizeX));
  j.Field('resizeY', ToStr(item.ResizeY));
  j.Field('resizeZ', ToStr(item.ResizeZ));

  j.Field('xan2d', ToStr(item.XAn2D));
  j.Field('yan2d', ToStr(item.YAn2D));
  j.Field('zan2d', ToStr(item.ZAn2D));

  j.Field('cost', ToStr(item.Cost));
  j.Field('stackable', ToStr(item.Stackable));
  j.Field('inventoryModel', ToStr(item.InventoryModel));

  j.Field('tradeable', _JsonBool(item.Tradeable));
  j.Field('geTradeable', _JsonBool(item.GeTradeable));

  j.Field('wearPos1', ToStr(item.WearPos1));
  j.Field('wearPos2', ToStr(item.WearPos2));
  j.Field('wearPos3', ToStr(item.WearPos3));

  j.Field('members', _JsonBool(item.Members));

  // Opcodes 40/41 allocate both arrays together and never at length 0, so
  // "empty" and "null" coincide here; no committed entry carries a `[]`.
  if Length(item.ColorFind) > 0 then
  begin
    j.Key('colorFind');
    _JsonIntArray(j, item.ColorFind);
  end;
  if Length(item.ColorReplace) > 0 then
  begin
    j.Key('colorReplace');
    _JsonIntArray(j, item.ColorReplace);
  end;
  if Length(item.TextureFind) > 0 then
  begin
    j.Key('textureFind');
    _JsonIntArray(j, item.TextureFind);
  end;
  if Length(item.TextureReplace) > 0 then
  begin
    j.Key('textureReplace');
    _JsonIntArray(j, item.TextureReplace);
  end;

  j.Field('zoom2d', ToStr(item.Zoom2D));
  j.Field('xOffset2d', ToStr(item.XOffset2D));
  j.Field('yOffset2d', ToStr(item.YOffset2D));

  j.Field('ambient', ToStr(item.Ambient));
  j.Field('contrast', ToStr(item.Contrast));

  // Declaration order is countCo before countObj, which is the reverse of the
  // order opcode 100..109 fills them in.
  if Length(item.CountCo) > 0 then
  begin
    j.Key('countCo');
    _JsonIntArray(j, item.CountCo);
  end;
  if Length(item.CountObj) > 0 then
  begin
    j.Key('countObj');
    _JsonIntArray(j, item.CountObj);
  end;

  j.Key('groundOps');
  j.OpenObj();
    j.Key('ops');
    j.OpenArr();
    n := _ItemGroundOpsLength(item.Ops.Ops);
    for i := 0 to n - 1 do
      if item.Ops.Ops[i] = '' then j.Elem('null')
      else
      begin
        j.Sep();
        j.OpenObj();
        j.Field('text', JSONQuoteHTML(item.Ops.Ops[i]));
        j.CloseObj();
      end;
    j.CloseArr();

    j.Key('subOps');
    _ItemSubOps(j, item.Ops);
    j.Key('conditionalOps');
    _ItemConditionalOps(j, item.Ops.Conditionals);
    j.Key('conditionalSubOps');
    _ItemConditionalSubOps(j, item.Ops.ConditionalSubs);
  j.CloseObj();

  j.Key('interfaceOptions');
  _JsonSlotArray(j, item.InterfaceOptions);

  // String[5][] - the outer array is allocated whole, the rows lazily, so a
  // row this port left at length 0 is Java's null row.
  if Length(item.Subops) > 0 then
  begin
    j.Key('subops');
    j.OpenArr();
    for i := 0 to High(item.Subops) do
      if Length(item.Subops[i]) = 0 then j.Elem('null')
      else
      begin
        j.Sep();
        _JsonSlotArray(j, item.Subops[i]);
      end;
    j.CloseArr();
  end;

  j.Field('maleModel0', ToStr(item.MaleModel0));
  j.Field('maleModel1', ToStr(item.MaleModel1));
  j.Field('maleModel2', ToStr(item.MaleModel2));
  j.Field('maleOffset', ToStr(item.MaleOffset));
  j.Field('maleHeadModel', ToStr(item.MaleHeadModel));
  j.Field('maleHeadModel2', ToStr(item.MaleHeadModel2));

  j.Field('femaleModel0', ToStr(item.FemaleModel0));
  j.Field('femaleModel1', ToStr(item.FemaleModel1));
  j.Field('femaleModel2', ToStr(item.FemaleModel2));
  j.Field('femaleOffset', ToStr(item.FemaleOffset));
  j.Field('femaleHeadModel', ToStr(item.FemaleHeadModel));
  j.Field('femaleHeadModel2', ToStr(item.FemaleHeadModel2));

  j.Field('category', ToStr(item.Category));

  // The key really is `notedID` with a capital D - it is the Java field name.
  j.Field('notedID', ToStr(item.NotedId));
  j.Field('notedTemplate', ToStr(item.NotedTemplate));

  j.Field('team', ToStr(item.Team));
  j.Field('weight', ToStr(item.Weight));

  j.Field('shiftClickDropIndex', ToStr(item.ShiftClickDropIndex));

  j.Field('boughtId', ToStr(item.BoughtId));
  j.Field('boughtTemplateId', ToStr(item.BoughtTemplateId));

  j.Field('placeholderId', ToStr(item.PlaceholderId));
  j.Field('placeholderTemplateId', ToStr(item.PlaceholderTemplateId));

  if Length(item.Params.Ids) > 0 then
  begin
    ItemDefParamOrder(item.Params, order);
    j.Key('params');
    j.OpenObj();
    for i := 0 to High(order) do
    begin
      k := order[i];
      // Gson writes a Map<Integer,...> key as String.valueOf(key), and the
      // value through the runtime type's adapter: a bare number or a quoted,
      // escaped string. Never a float - nothing in this archive is one.
      j.Key(ToStr(item.Params.Ids[k]));
      if item.Params.IsString[k] then
        j.Text += JSONQuoteHTML(item.Params.Strings[k])
      else
        j.Text += ToStr(item.Params.Ints[k]);
    end;
    j.CloseObj();
  end;

  j.CloseObj();
  Result := j.Text;
end;

(*
## ItemDefJSONOf
```pascal
function ItemDefJSONOf(id: Integer; data: TByteArray): String;
```
Decodes one ITEM archive file and renders it, resolving the `''`-versus-null
ambiguity on `examine` and `unknown1` for you. `data` is the file's bytes as
`TCacheStore.ReadFiles` returns them and `id` is its **file id**, which is the
item id and not its position in that array.

The probe decode only runs when one of the two fields came back empty, and it
costs a second `TItemDefinition.Load` when it does. See the unit docstring for
why it is exact rather than a heuristic.

```pascal
files := store.ReadFiles(Ord(EIndexType.CONFIGS), Ord(EConfigType.ITEM), []);
WriteLn(ItemDefJSONOf(4151, files[FilePositionOf(table, Ord(EConfigType.ITEM), 4151)]));
```
*)
function ItemDefJSONOf(id: Integer; data: TByteArray): String;
var
  item, probe: TItemDefinition;
  hasExamine, hasUnknown1: Boolean;
  probed: TByteArray;
begin
  item := TItemDefinition.Load(id, data);

  hasExamine := item.Examine <> '';
  hasUnknown1 := item.Unknown1 <> '';

  if (not hasExamine) or (not hasUnknown1) then
  begin
    // Two complete opcodes: 3 (examine) and 9 (unknown1), each carrying the
    // one-character string '*' terminated by 0. The stream stays exactly
    // consumed, so the loader's end-of-data check still holds.
    probed := [3, ITEMDEF_PROBE_BYTE, 0, 9, ITEMDEF_PROBE_BYTE, 0] + data;
    probe := TItemDefinition.Load(id, probed);
    if not hasExamine then hasExamine := probe.Examine <> ITEMDEF_PROBE_MARK;
    if not hasUnknown1 then hasUnknown1 := probe.Unknown1 <> ITEMDEF_PROBE_MARK;
  end;

  Result := ItemDefJSON(item, hasExamine, hasUnknown1);
end;

// A String as its raw bytes. `Result := []` first because SetLength keeps the
// overlapping prefix of whatever the slot held before.
function _ItemDefBytes(s: String): TByteArray;
begin
  Result := [];
  if s = '' then Exit;
  SetLength(Result, Length(s));
  Move(s[1], Result[0], Length(s));
end;


(*
## DumpItemDefinitions
```pascal
procedure DumpItemDefinitions(var store: TCacheStore; zipPath: String);
```
Writes the whole `jsons/item-definitions.zip` for `store`: every file of the
CONFIGS/ITEM archive, unfiltered, named `<id>.json`, in byte-lexicographic entry
order.

**The container's bytes will not match the committed zip and are not meant
to.** Simba's deflate compresses harder than the JDK's and `TZipWriter` pins its
own timestamps, so verification is per-entry content - which
`dev/tests/411_itemdefs.simba` does against all 33745 committed entries.

```pascal
store := TCacheStore.Create(cacheDir);
DumpItemDefinitions(store, dump.Path('jsons/item-definitions.zip'));
```
*)
procedure DumpItemDefinitions(var store: TCacheStore; zipPath: String);
var
  files: array of TByteArray;
  table: TCacheRefTable;
  ids, order: TIntegerArray;
  names: TStringArray;
  zip: TZipWriter;
  a, i, k: Integer;
begin
  table := store.GetRefTable(Ord(EIndexType.CONFIGS));
  a := table.IndexOfArchive(Ord(EConfigType.ITEM));
  if a = -1 then
    raise 'DumpItemDefs: this cache has no CONFIGS/ITEM archive';

  ids := table.Archives[a].FileIds;
  files := store.ReadFiles(Ord(EIndexType.CONFIGS), Ord(EConfigType.ITEM), []);
  if Length(files) <> Length(ids) then
    raise 'DumpItemDefs: the ITEM archive holds ' + ToStr(Length(files)) +
          ' files but its reference table names ' + ToStr(Length(ids));

  SetLength(names, Length(ids));
  for i := 0 to High(ids) do
    names[i] := ToStr(ids[i]) + '.json';
  order := DumpEntryOrder(names);

  zip.Init();
  for i := 0 to High(order) do
  begin
    k := order[i];
    // One document at a time: the whole archive is 132 MB of json, and the
    // writer already holds every deflated payload.
    zip.AddBytes(names[k], _ItemDefBytes(ItemDefJSONOf(ids[k], files[k])));
  end;

  zip.Save(zipPath);
end;
